Point-of-use contamination is a system problem
Chemicals may leave the factory at the required purity and acquire contaminants in pipes, valves, containers or storage tanks. Testing needs to cover the whole delivery route to the wet-processing tool. For tightly controlled nodes, filtration, compatible packaging and rapid analytical feedback become part of the service that supports the chemistry itself.

Selective cleaning can protect device yield
A remover that attacks a target residue too slowly may leave defects, while a stronger solution can damage adjacent metals or dielectric films. Matching chemistry to process temperature, contact time and subsequent rinse conditions requires experiment data. This complexity favors application engineers who can validate a formulation in the real process stack instead of relying on laboratory etch rates alone.

